/*!
    \example simple_service_client
    \title Simple Service Client
    \ingroup qtros2-examples
    \meta sortkey {40}
    \brief Calling a std_srvs/SetBool service declaratively from QML by
    binding the request property.

    \c{Simple Service Client} is the calling side of the \l{Simple Service}
    example: it wraps one \l{Qt ROS2 std_srvs QML Types}{SetBoolServiceClient}
    in a \l{Qt ROS2 Core QML Types}{Node} and keeps a \c std_srvs/SetBool
    service in sync with a switch — the switch expresses the \e desired lamp
    state, and the service response reports what the server actually did.

    \image simple_service_client/simple_service_client.png "The Simple Service Client window: an editable topic, the lamp switch, and the last response."

    \section1 Running the example

    \list 1
        \li Source your ROS 2 environment, then launch Qt Creator from the same
            shell so it can find the generated QML modules.
        \li Open \c examples/simple_service_client/CMakeLists.txt in Qt Creator,
            then build and run.
        \li Provide the service by running the \l{Simple Service} example with
            the same topic.
    \endlist

    \section1 Calling a service by binding

    A service client is declared as a child of a \c Node. Binding the
    \c request property is all it takes to call the service: every change of
    the bound value schedules a call (multiple changes per event-loop
    iteration coalesce into one), and the result lands in the read-only
    \c response property:

    \qml
    import QtRos2.StdSrvs

    Node {
        id: rosNode
        nodeName: "simple_service_client_node"

        SetBoolServiceClient {
            id: lampClient
            topic: topicField.text
            request: lampSwitch.checked
        }
    }
    \endqml

    \qml
    Label {
        text: lampClient.response.message
    }
    \endqml

    The \c request binding behaves like desired state that is reconciled with
    the server: if the service is not available yet, or a call is still in
    flight, the latest value is remembered and dispatched as soon as possible.
    Flip the switch before starting the server and the state is applied the
    moment the server appears. This behavior is controlled by
    \l {ServiceClientBase::autoCall}{autoCall} (on by default; auto-calls only
    happen when \c request is actually written).

    Run it against \l{Simple Service} and burn the bulb out: the response
    switches to \c{success: false} with an explanatory message — service
    failure is in-band, part of the response value, so it is bindable state
    like everything else.

    \section1 Imperative calls

    \c callService() remains for one-shot, imperative calls; it returns a
    JavaScript promise:

    \qml
    lampClient.callService(true).then(response => {
        console.log(response.success, response.message)
    }, error => {
        console.log("call failed:", error)
    })
    \endqml

    Either way, \c isServiceReady reports whether a server was discovered on
    the graph, \c isCallPending is \c true while a response is awaited, and
    transport-level failures (the server disappearing mid-call) are reported
    through the \c callFailed signal and the promise rejection.

    \sa {Simple Service}, {Qt ROS2 std_srvs QML Types}
*/
